WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.203
The historical Uber case begins with rider and
driver applications polling for changing trip state.

00:00:06.203 --> 00:00:12.405
The slides report that polling dominated gateway
traffic and that startup requests competed on weak

00:00:12.405 --> 00:00:13.408
mobile networks.

00:00:13.408 --> 00:00:19.109
RAMEN separates the decision to send an update from
creating its content and delivering it.

00:00:19.109 --> 00:00:25.062
This division targets both operational efficiency
and the difficulty of migrating many existing

00:00:25.062 --> 00:00:25.625
features.

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:32.092
RAMEN attaches delivery policy to payloads: priority
determines which pending updates matter first,

00:00:32.092 --> 00:00:38.625
expiry limits how long an update remains useful, and
replacement can retain the latest state instead

00:00:38.625 --> 00:00:40.585
of every intermediate version.

00:00:40.585 --> 00:00:47.052
The historical SSE protocol adds sequence numbers,
reconnect cursors and separate acknowledgements.

00:00:47.052 --> 00:00:53.192
These application mechanisms supply recovery
behavior that a persistent transport alone cannot

00:00:53.192 --> 00:00:54.629
guarantee to the user.
